{"parentUuid":"c184395f-acc4-42df-a0af-08f6425e76ff","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"af05282ff225ef9db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011pVycbRHARHf2Dzwk5EttN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"GNN learning to branch sample efficiency small dataset 2024 finetune pretrained\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"gnn-pretrain\",\"url\":\"https://snap.stanford.edu/gnn-pretrain/\"},{\"title\":\"Fine-Tuning Pretrained GNN Models in PyTorch for Specialized Tasks - Sling Academy\",\"url\":\"https://www.slingacademy.com/article/fine-tuning-pretrained-gnn-models-in-pytorch-for-specialized-tasks/\"},{\"title\":\"Graph-Aware Language Model Pre-Training on a Large Graph Corpus Can Help Multiple Graph Applications - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10876412/\"},{\"title\":\"[2308.06960] Search to Fine-tune Pre-trained Graph Neural Networks for Graph-level Tasks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06960\"},{\"title\":\"Strategies for Pre-training Graph Neural Networks | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=HJlWWJSFDH\"},{\"title\":\"Transfer Learning in Scalable Graph Neural Network for Improved Physical Simulation - Apple Machine Learning Research\",\"url\":\"https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/scalable-graph-neural-network\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2020 STRATEGIES\",\"url\":\"https://cs.stanford.edu/people/jure/pubs/pretrain-iclr20.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Quick-Tune: Quickly Learning Which Pretrained Model to Finetune and How\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2306.03828v4\"},{\"title\":\"Get more for less: Principled Data Selection for Warming Up Fine-Tuning in LLMs | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=QmYNBVukex\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Effective and Generalizable Fine-tuning for Pre- ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2022.02.03.479055v1.full.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found several relevant papers and resources related to GNN (Graph Neural Network) pretraining, fine-tuning, and transfer learning. Here are the key findings:\n\n## GNN Pretraining and Fine-tuning Strategies\n\nAn effective solution to challenges with limited labeled data is to pre-train a model on related tasks where data is abundant, then fine-tune it on a downstream task, and researchers have developed strategies for pre-training Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and systematically studied their effectiveness on multiple datasets and GNN architectures.\n\n## Search-Based Fine-tuning Approach (2024)\n\nA 2024 approach proposes searching to fine-tune pre-trained graph neural networks for graph-level tasks (S2PGNN), which adaptively designs a suitable fine-tuning framework for the given labeled data on the downstream task. The empirical studies show that S2PGNN can be implemented on top of 10 famous pre-trained GNNs and consistently improve their performance, achieving better performance than existing fine-tuning strategies.\n\n## Handling Small Datasets\n\nPre-training GNNs leads to orders-of-magnitude faster training and convergence in the fine-tuning stage, and the most expressive architecture (GIN) benefits more from pre-tuning compared to less expressive architectures.\n\n## Key Challenges\n\nWhen ad-hoc strategies are applied, pre-trained GNNs often exhibit negative transfer and perform worse than non-pre-trained GNNs on many downstream tasks. This highlights the importance of careful fine-tuning design when working with limited downstream data.\n\nThe search results contain multiple 2024 publications and resources on this topic, though the specific focus on \"learning to branch\" optimization in branching algorithms combined with GNN fine-tuning on small datasets wasn't directly captured in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"01081fdf-18ac-4d67-ba7c-959df075ac74","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:15.772Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c184395f-acc4-42df-a0af-08f6425e76ff","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3733d04d-7b90-41e0-83b3-a2b2f760d887","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"af05282ff225ef9db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RjpoyK4asNmeFsndgfVk4j","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Ecole gasse learning branch CP-SAT primal heuristic neural diving 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2024\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=joMMM9eadc\"},{\"title\":\"[2107.10201] Learning a Large Neighborhood Search Algorithm for Mixed Integer Programs\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2107.10201\"},{\"title\":\"Confidence Threshold Neural Diving - Ecole\",\"url\":\"https://www.ecole.ai/2021/ml4co-competition/proceedings/1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Learning a Large Neighborhood Search Algorithm for Mixed Integer Programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.10201v1\"},{\"title\":\"ExCoS @ CP/SAT 2025\",\"url\":\"https://sites.google.com/view/excos2025\"},{\"title\":\"Primal Heuristics for Branch and Price: The Assets of Diving Methods | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2018.0822\"},{\"title\":\"1 © 2024 Fair Isaac Corporation. Confidential. This presentation is provided\",\"url\":\"https://co-at-work.zib.de/slides/COatWork2024_Berthold_MLinMIP.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Primal Heuristics for Branch and Price: The Assets of Diving Methods\",\"url\":\"https://ideas.repec.org/a/inm/orijoc/v31y2019i2p251-267.html\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Exam Syllabus 2024: Updated Section-Wise Syllabus (Download PDF) - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/sat/sat-syllabus/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding your query about Ecole, learning branch strategies, CP-SAT, primal heuristics, and neural diving:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Learning-Based Branch and Primal Heuristic Research:**\n\nGasse et al. (2019) proposed modeling an IP instance using a bipartite graph and extracting variable representations using Graph Convolutional Networks (GCN), which appears to be foundational work in this area.\n\n**Neural Diving:**\n\nNeural Diving, as described in Nair et al. (2020), achieved previous best primal gap results on various benchmark datasets. This approach utilizes graph neural networks to predict the value of each variable, then searches for the best feasible solution within a trust region constructed by the predicted partial solutions.\n\n**Ecole Library:**\n\nFeatures are extracted via the Ecole library as in Gasse et al. (2019), indicating that Ecole is a tool used for extracting features from optimization problems for machine learning approaches.\n\n**Recent Work (2024-2025):**\n\nThe search results show ongoing research in machine learning for combinatorial optimization through 2024 and 2025, with publications at major venues like ICLR, NeurIPS, and ICML, though the results don't contain specific papers solely focused on the exact combination of terms you mentioned (CP-SAT with the specific combination of all those techniques).\n\nIf you need more specific information about recent papers combining these exact components, a more targeted search on academic databases might yield additional results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9df6667b-a800-4266-b2c0-0b694614a4ed","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:16.159Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3733d04d-7b90-41e0-83b3-a2b2f760d887","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2f3f7879-ae43-4893-8a03-a0dc138bb90f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"af05282ff225ef9db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRXq4YPhvVh8Xf1Aa2VKjJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"end-to-end\" neural CO solver TSP vehicle routing benchmark 2025 limitations\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/b210c387381713a14a4f5a607aff3520-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MEMENTO: Memory-Enhanced Neural Solvers for Routing Problems | InstaDeep - Decision-Making AI For The Enterprise\",\"url\":\"https://instadeep.com/2025/11/memento-memory-enhanced-neural-solvers-for-routing-problems/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co: Awesome machine learning for combinatorial optimization papers. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Thinklab-SJTU/awesome-ml4co\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization Algorithms for Solving Vehicle Routing Problems: A Comprehensive Survey with Perspectives\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2406.00415v1\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - dongjinkun/COPZoo: Neural Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/dongjinkun/COPZoo\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Neural Combinatorial Optimization Solver for the Min-max Heterogeneous Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.21386\"},{\"title\":\"Boosting Neural Combinatorial Optimization for Large-Scale Vehicle Routing Problems | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=TbTJJNjumY\"},{\"title\":\"URS: A Unified Neural Routing Solver for Cross-Problem Zero-Shot Generalization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.23413v1\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization for Robust Routing Problem with Uncertain Travel Times | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=DoewNm2uT3\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Generalization of Neural Combinatorial Optimization for Vehicle Routing Problems via Test-Time Projection Learning | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=QPJjiNCRq1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about end-to-end neural combinatorial optimization (CO) solvers for TSP and vehicle routing problems in 2025, including their limitations. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Key Limitations of End-to-End Neural CO Solvers (2025)\n\n**Generalization Issues:**\nWhile existing NCO methods trained on small-scale instances (e.g., 100 nodes) have demonstrated considerable success on problems of similar scale, their performance significantly degrades when applied to large-scale scenarios due to distributional shift between training and testing data.\n\n**Performance Dependency:**\nA limitation of AGFN (a 2025 neural solver) is that its performance heavily relies on the generator's ability to produce diverse, high-quality solutions, which may account for its slightly lower test performance on 200-node CVRP and TSP instances.\n\n**Applicability Constraints:**\nExisting NCO solvers exhibit poor cross-scale and cross-distribution generalization abilities and cannot solve large-scale VRPs in real-time. Additionally, their applicability remains largely confined to TSP and CVRP, with limited success on more complex VRP variants.\n\n**Problem Scope Limitations:**\nMost solvers cannot simultaneously address all or most types of VRP variants. Therefore, researchers must develop specialized NCO solvers to address VRP variants with additional constraints.\n\nThese limitations indicate that while end-to-end neural CO solvers show promise on benchmark problems, they struggle with scalability, generalization across problem sizes and distributions, and applicability to diverse VRP variants.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"234b37f2-a2d0-43bf-be2c-5bd301adb9a1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:16.612Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2f3f7879-ae43-4893-8a03-a0dc138bb90f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
